WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
[Soft instrumental music]
It's six in the morning.

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:05.125
Across the facility,
another day is beginning.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.480
Employees are arriving.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.935
Equipment needs checking.

00:00:09.425 --> 00:00:11.899
In the kitchen,
ingredients are being prepared,

00:00:12.205 --> 00:00:15.105
and supplies are making their way
throughout the facility.

00:00:15.535 --> 00:00:17.693
Breakfast service begins at seven-thirty.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.070
There's a lot to get done before then.

00:00:20.875 --> 00:00:22.610
Every role has the part to play.

00:00:23.255 --> 00:00:24.781
Cooks are starting breakfast.

00:00:24.905 --> 00:00:27.120
Servers are preparing their stations.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.278
And supervisors are confirming coverage
across the building.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.405
Each responsibility
has its own time and place.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.248
But the plan doesn't always go perfectly.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.365
Someone calls off.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.113
One serving location is suddenly short.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:44.555
With a few clicks,
the supervisor reviews the gap

00:00:44.555 --> 00:00:46.450
and reassigns an available employee.

00:00:47.135 --> 00:00:49.503
Over in the kitchen,
there's another problem.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.669
A walk-in refrigerator
isn't holding temperature.

00:00:53.375 --> 00:00:54.465
Forty-eight degrees.

00:00:55.025 --> 00:00:56.551
Outside its configured range.

00:00:57.325 --> 00:01:00.957
The employee records the reading
and documents the corrective action.

00:01:01.235 --> 00:01:04.040
The supervisor marks the refrigerator
out of service,

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.180
while the team follows
its food-safety procedures

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.864
and moves to an approved backup.

00:01:08.475 --> 00:01:11.065
A maintenance request
goes to Plant Operations,

00:01:11.065 --> 00:01:14.740
where a supervisor reviews the problem
and creates a work order for the repair.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:15.970
Plans change.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:17.560
Teams respond.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.541
And the work stays visible.

00:01:20.345 --> 00:01:21.713
That's why we built Vssyl.

00:01:22.555 --> 00:01:24.655
Employees can see where they're assigned,

00:01:24.655 --> 00:01:26.995
what matters now, and what comes next.

00:01:27.755 --> 00:01:31.080
Supervisors can see coverage,
equipment needing attention,

00:01:31.575 --> 00:01:34.417
maintenance requests,
and upcoming service milestones.

00:01:35.025 --> 00:01:38.236
And managers can see
how the entire operation is progressing,

00:01:38.395 --> 00:01:40.184
without chasing down every update.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:41.800
It's nine-thirty.

00:01:42.505 --> 00:01:44.084
Breakfast service is complete.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:47.170
The team's completed work
and confirmed the morning milestones

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.907
remain part of the day's history.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.197
But the operation doesn't stop there.

00:01:51.925 --> 00:01:54.720
Lunch is approaching,
bringing a new service cycle,

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.480
new responsibilities, and new deadlines.

00:01:58.265 --> 00:02:00.190
And while the operation moves forward,

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.945
outstanding issues carry over
until they're resolved.

00:02:02.945 --> 00:02:06.785
Because running a facility
isn't simply about completing tasks.

00:02:06.935 --> 00:02:09.356
It's about keeping everything
moving together.

00:02:10.145 --> 00:02:11.566
That's what Vssyl connects.

00:02:12.095 --> 00:02:13.090
People.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.005
Places.

00:02:14.255 --> 00:02:15.155
Work.

00:02:15.395 --> 00:02:16.295
Time.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.400
A clearer way to run the day.
